RDCFG_INDEP    t1      CFG-DEP   1          319.96      4.968   1.0000

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: the wrong-condition control (slots 20/21, TRISC_CFG)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  variant thr    rdcfg/pair    base/pair   difference
  t1      1           3.968        2.968       +1.000

TTBENCH_CFGLAT_WRONGBIT: 1.0000
  TRISC_CFG is about the RISCV core's outstanding memory requests, not
  about the Configuration Unit's pipeline, so ~0 here is the CORRECT
  reading and is what makes the C12 difference above attributable to the
  condition bit. A control that moved would mean it is not.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: RDCFG -> GPR -> consumer, dependent against independent (26/27)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Both arms are TTI_RDCFG(60,0) followed by TTI_ADDDMAREG(1,63,0,X).
  X = 60 in slot 26 (the RDCFG destination) and X = 59 in slot 27.
  BlackholeA0 RDCFG.md makes the separation software's job, so the
  documented prediction is that this difference is ZERO and the latency
  is not a duration at all. See the visibility section below.

  variant thr      dep/pair   indep/pair   difference
  t1      1           4.969        4.968       +0.001

TTBENCH_DEP_DIFF: 0.0006
TTBENCH_DEP_PARTS: 4.9688 1.9682 2.9719
  the dependent pair costs 4.969 cycles against 1.968 + 2.972 = 4.940 for
  the two instructions measured bare (probes 14 and 10). A pair that
  came in UNDER the sum would mean an arm is not issuing both
  instructions, and then a zero difference would say nothing.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase A: RDCFG's latency as a producer-to-consumer DISTANCE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  seeds 0xA5A5A5A5 / 0x5A5A5A5A, never-ran marker 0xDEADBEEF, config index 0,
  64 repetitions per separation.

  control: no RDCFG at all  -> 0xA5A5A5A5, 0x5A5A5A5A (want 0xA5A5A5A5, 0x5A5A5A5A)  ok
  control: RDCFG at d=8     -> 0x00000000, 0x00000000 (want them equal, and
                               neither seed nor marker)                ok
  consumer never ran: 0 repetitions;  unexplained: 0

  d         fresh      stale      other
  1            64          0          0
  2            64          0          0
  3            64          0          0
  4            64          0          0

TTBENCH_VIS_CONTROLS: stale-ok fresh-ok 0 0
TTBENCH_VIS_DMIN: 1 64
TTBENCH_VIS_COUNTS: 64 64 64 64
  d_min is the smallest separation at which EVERY repetition saw the value
  RDCFG wrote. Because the consumer may read its operand some cycles into
  its own execution, d_min is a LOWER BOUND on the latency -- which is the
  direction the charging policy takes bounds in. d_min = 2 corroborates
  ConfigurationUnit.md's '>= 2 cycles' exactly; d_min = 1 would leave the
  '>= 2' unreached rather than refute it, since a consumer that reads late
  is a complete alternative explanation. A MIXTURE at any d (fresh and
  stale both non-zero) means the RISC-V front end did not deliver the
  sequence at one instruction per cycle and the separation is not what it
  says; that is why every repetition is counted rather than one taken.

